{"items":[{"id":"article-waiting-on-the-lord","kind":"article","title":"Learning to Wait on the Lord","titleZh":"学习仰望耶和华","date":"2026-06-10","author":"Pastor David Chen","excerpt":"In a culture that prizes speed, waiting on God can feel like wasted time. Here is what a season of waiting actually built in me.","body":"In a culture that prizes speed, waiting on God can feel like wasted time. I spent most of last year waiting on an answer I thought I needed immediately — and God gave me something else instead: patience I didn't know I lacked.\n\nWaiting is not the absence of God's work. It's often where the deepest work happens, quietly, underground, long before anything visible changes. The Israelites waited forty years in the wilderness. David waited years between being anointed and being crowned. Waiting was never wasted time in Scripture — it was formation.\n\nIf you're in a season of waiting right now, I want to encourage you: keep showing up. Keep praying. Keep serving. The waiting is not a pause in your story — it's a chapter in it."},{"id":"article-two-languages","kind":"article","title":"Why We Sing in Two Languages","titleZh":"我们为何用双语敬拜","date":"2026-05-02","author":"Pastor Michael Wong","excerpt":"Bilingual worship is not a compromise between generations — it is a picture of the family God has actually given us.","body":"Visitors sometimes ask why our Sunday worship moves between English and Chinese in the same set, sometimes in the same song. It isn't a compromise between generations — it's a picture of the family God has actually given us.\n\nOur congregation spans immigrants who still dream in Mandarin and children who barely remember a word of it. Singing in both languages means no one has to leave part of themselves at the door to belong here. It also means we get to practice something harder than translation: patience with each other, and trust that the Spirit understands every tongue in the room even when we don't.\n\nWe're not aiming for perfect symmetry between the two languages. We're aiming for a room where everyone can say, in whichever language reaches them first, that they were truly worshipping — not just present."},{"id":"testimony-family-in-flushing","kind":"testimony","title":"Finding a Family in Flushing","titleZh":"在法拉盛找到属灵的家","date":"2026-04-18","author":"Anna Kim","excerpt":"I moved to Flushing knowing no one. Two years later, I can’t imagine my life without this church family.","body":"I moved to Flushing knowing no one. I had a job and an apartment, but no community — and I told myself that was fine, that I'd manage on my own like I always had.\n\nA coworker invited me to a Sunday service almost as an afterthought, and I only went because I didn't have a good excuse not to. What I found wasn't a polished production — it was a room full of people who actually wanted to know my name the second time I came back, and the third.\n\nTwo years later, I can't imagine my life without this church family. They've prayed with me through a layoff, celebrated a promotion with me, and shown up to help me move apartments twice. I came for a Sunday service. I stayed because I finally stopped managing on my own."},{"id":"article-workers-for-the-harvest","kind":"article","title":"Preparing Workers for the Harvest","titleZh":"预备收割的工人","date":"2026-03-20","author":"Pastor David Chen","excerpt":"BCC411 was never meant to just gather people. From the beginning, the vision was to send them.","body":"BCC411 was never meant to just gather people on a Sunday morning. From the beginning, the vision behind our name has been about a harvest — and a harvest needs workers, not just spectators.\n\nThat's why so much of what we do outside of Sunday service is quietly aimed at formation: small groups that ask hard questions, retreats that slow people down long enough to hear from God, and a culture where \"what is God calling you to do next\" is a normal dinner-table question, not an awkward one.\n\nFour churches on two continents didn't happen because a handful of pastors worked harder. It happened because ordinary members took seriously the idea that they, too, were being prepared to go somewhere and do something. We hope the same is true of you."},{"id":"testimony-doubt-to-trust","kind":"testimony","title":"From Doubt to Trust","titleZh":"从怀疑到信靠","date":"2026-02-14","author":"James Liu","excerpt":"I spent years quietly unsure whether any of this was true. What changed wasn’t an argument — it was a season of being seen.","body":"I grew up going to church, but by my twenties I was quietly unsure whether any of it was actually true. I kept showing up out of habit more than conviction, and I assumed most people in the room had it more figured out than I did.\n\nWhat changed wasn't a clever argument that finally convinced me. It was a small group leader who noticed I'd gone quiet and asked, privately, if I was doing okay — and actually waited for an honest answer instead of a polite one. For the first time, my doubts were met with patience instead of a quick fix.\n\nI'm not going to pretend every question I had got answered. Some are still open. But I learned that faith doesn't require having no doubts — it requires bringing them somewhere safe. I found that here."},{"id":"article-gratitude-practices","kind":"article","title":"Five Ways to Practice Gratitude This Season","titleZh":"感恩的五个操练","date":"2026-01-10","author":"Grace Liu","excerpt":"Gratitude rarely just happens to us. Like most things worth having, it has to be practiced.","body":"Gratitude rarely just happens to us. Like most things worth having, it has to be practiced — especially in a season that hasn't gone the way we hoped.\n\nHere are five small habits our small group has been trying this year: write down three specific things each night, not general categories. Thank a person directly instead of just thinking it. Say a prayer of thanks before asking for anything else. Notice one ordinary thing — a meal, a commute, a conversation — you'd normally overlook. And finally, share what you're grateful for out loud with someone else; gratitude spoken tends to multiply.\n\nNone of these are complicated. What they have in common is that they turn gratitude from a feeling we wait for into a practice we choose, on the good days and the hard ones alike."},{"id":"testimony-healing-after-loss","kind":"testimony","title":"Healing After Loss","titleZh":"失落后的医治","date":"2025-12-01","author":"Grace Tan","excerpt":"After my father passed, I stopped believing grief and faith could sit in the same room. This church taught me they could.","body":"After my father passed, I stopped believing grief and faith could sit in the same room. I thought I was supposed to be comforted quickly, and when I wasn't, I assumed something was wrong with my faith instead of my circumstances.\n\nWhat I found here was different. No one rushed me toward being okay. People sat with me in the hard months — bringing meals without being asked, remembering the date of his passing the following year without me having to mention it, praying prayers that made room for anger and sadness instead of skipping past them.\n\nI still miss my father every day. But I no longer believe grief and faith are opposites. This community taught me they can hold hands — and that healing is slower, and more communal, than I ever expected."},{"id":"testimony-new-start-houston","kind":"testimony","title":"A New Start in Houston","titleZh":"在休斯顿的新开始","date":"2025-10-05","author":"Daniel Wu","excerpt":"A cross-country move for work turned into the first church I ever chose for myself, rather than inherited.","body":"A job relocated me to Houston with about three weeks' notice. I packed my life into a truck and told myself finding a church could wait until I was settled — which, if I'm honest, usually means it never happens.\n\nA former coworker who'd moved a year earlier gave me one name before I left: the Houston congregation of BCC411. I went the first Sunday mostly out of loneliness, not conviction. It was the first church I ever chose for myself, rather than the one I simply grew up attending.\n\nA year later, I lead a small group of my own — mostly other transplants who arrived the same way I did, alone and unsure. Houston didn't just give me a job. It gave me a church I actually chose, and now I get to be the familiar face for someone else's first Sunday."}]}